as the losses against  the rate of inflation which has been  said is 11 at the moment so it's not  just a losing out against the cost of  living they're actually proposing to cut  pay on the railway going forward that  was a proposal to put to us by the train  operating companies on thursday and  since that time they've not invited us  to any meetings whatsoever just just to  be clear on this because the reports  that i've read uh are saying uh that the  train offering 18 companies are offering  around two point five percent because no  offer and you uh are gunning for uh  eleven percent pay wise is that true  neither of those things is true what is  true the train operating companies have  made no offer at all right during these  talks there are two strands of talks  network rail have got a strand which is  about half the people and the train  operators the train operators adjourn  the meeting on thursday they've not  invited us to any talks whatsoever and  they've made no offer on pay and what  they told us is what they plan to do and  what are you looking for on pay we're  looking for a pay rise that reflects the  cost of living so what does that mean  well that's the choice let's be really  clear what are you asking for at the  time of the network rail pay deal that  should have been done in december  it was 7.1 percent the retail price  index let's not forget that those  members didn't get a pay rise last year  either so is that what you're looking  for that's what we that's what the cost  of living would have been at the time  these deals should have been struck so  we're going to negotiate to see if we  can get a deal that reflects that cost  of living there are a number of ways  which you can put value into a package  it's not all about straightforward pay  so we'll talk to them constructively but  they're making offers that are nowhere  near there and for half the people in  the distribute there's no offer at all  and for many of them it's the third year  where there's no offer and no proposal  now grant shaps makes a point about the  amount of taxpayers money that is going  into the railways currently right and of  course we know through kovid that uh  taxpayers have to bankroll uh the  railways  and also less people go into the office  and so uh around 75 percent i think is  the sort of capacity on the railways he  says you know the government has spent  uh 16 billion pounds on railways and  start the pandemic 600 pounds per  household are you not effectively asking  taxpayers to bankroll salaries we're not  that 16 billion was at the peak of covid  where there are only three or four  percent of people traveling on the  railway our members worked through that  and they the government insisted that  the railways be kept open we worked with  them on that and that was the last time  i met grant chaps to talk about working  practices and the way that we could keep  the railway open so when they say we  didn't get furlough the reason we didn't  get furloughed is we kept working every  day every shift seven days a week the  point he's making is that actually we're  talking about taxpayers i'll come to  that so at that time there was there was  a subsidy but let's not forget during  that period these train operating  companies and the rolling stock leasing  companies and the subcontractors made  500 million pounds of profit in the  worst year for rail revenue so what  grant chaps is actually doing is  subsidising private sector profit  dividends and shares two of the  companies that i'm dealing with in this  dispute first group and go ahead are  subject to takeover bids of billions of  pounds they're really attractive to  private sector investors and they want  to take them over because they know that  there's going to be profit even while  the railway's subsidised it's not  actually true that they have been  subsidised to that level before kovid  they were subsidised by 6.4 billion and  then in the covered period they had to  add to that to keep it open because  there was no revenue and what these  trade operating companies said was if  you don't give us money for the  emergency measures we're going to give  you the keys back so that the state can  run the railway and that would have been  a more efficient way to run it actually  because that would have saved 500  million pounds of profit that's going  out to these companies so you can't have  it both ways he's claiming that uh  workers are subsidised while he's  subsidising dividends and shares well um  do you ever worry  with the big strikes happening  that there's a risk that you're over  playing your hand grant shaps has said  and he said the program earlier that  workers shouldn't risk striking  themselves out of a job we know that the  government is looking at changing some  of the legislation around strike so that  you know it's easier for workers to come  in to cover uh striking workers also  potentially increasing the damages that  companies can get back from unions for  illegal strikes do you worry we don't  take part in illegal strikes this is a  lawful ballot conducted under this tory  government's anti-union legislation but  do you think that you might be  overplaying your hand and you're  basically going to battle with the  government and there will be a backlash  well if we don't play our hand thousands  of my members will lose their jobs  railway services will be cut back the  safety regime that has been in place for  a good deal of time will be cut back 50  percent of the maintenance on the  railway infrastructure is scheduled to  be cut so that he can cut three thousand  of our members jobs so we have to fight  this because we haven't had any pay  rises  we're faced with thousands of job cuts  and they want to rip up our terms and  conditions in a form of fire and re-hire  that's internal to the railway it's just  as ruthless as p o really but they  haven't got agency workers to come in so  they're saying to our pe